WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.610
There's a funny thing that sort of quietly
happening, which is that more and more republicans

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:06.821
are just quitting politics in order harder

00:00:06.821 --> 00:00:13.000
to at least in part, get away from a Republican
party that's led by Donald Trump and which

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.610
might actually reelect Donald Trump to a second
term.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:19.210
The latest is Texas Congressman Mike Conaway.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:22.980
He's announced that he's just not going to
try and get reelected in 2020 he's going by

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:28.150
by, and officially he hasn't said yet that
this is about Trump necessarily, but we have

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:33.590
other Republicans on record saying that serving
under Donald Trump is an embarrassment.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:37.989
We've got former Republican congressman Tom
Davis who told the hill, quote, serving in

00:00:37.989 --> 00:00:40.440
the era of Trump has fewer awards.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.400
He has made an already hostile political environment.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:49.450
Worse every day there is some indefensible
tweet or comment to defend or explain.

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:53.070
It is exhausting and often embarrassing.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:58.470
We already heard from Paul Mitchell, Pete
Olson, Martha Roby, rob Bishop, Republican

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:00.579
members of Congress that they're just bailing.

00:01:00.579 --> 00:01:02.239
They're going away, they're quitting.

00:01:02.239 --> 00:01:07.970
You'll remember on the Senate side back in
2018 that Republican Senator Jeff Flake said

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:13.350
he was bailing out after very public spats
with Donald Trump, may have even been 2017

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.960
hard to keep track of how quickly things are
happening.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:22.470
That seat was ultimately lost by Republicans
a and was won by Democratic senator, now Democratic

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.200
senator, Kirsten Cinema.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:28.800
So there are probably some additional opportunities
for Democrats as a result of this.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:34.460
You, you've got of the five Republican members
of Congress that are retiring for our in pretty

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:39.230
safe Republican districts, Pete Olsen's district
is maybe winnable.

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:42.880
And there's also the possibility that these
Republican members of Congress just kind of

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:48.780
realized that Democrats are probably keeping
control of the house in 2020 maybe even extending

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.210
their control.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:54.990
They're not thrilled with the idea of another
two years out of power, particularly under

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:55.990
Donald Trump.

00:01:55.990 --> 00:02:00.799
And so in total, it's not because of Trump
alone, they're just going to bail.

00:02:00.799 --> 00:02:06.009
And there's something funny about this, which
is that we've heard rumblings for months about

00:02:06.009 --> 00:02:10.840
this, about Republicans who don't want to
be serving under Donald Trump, but if there

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:15.670
were enough of them with a spine instead of
quitting, they could get together and actually

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:21.180
push back against Donald Trump, sort of acting
like the check and balance to the White House

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:26.680
that they're supposed to be instead of merely
being subservient, and I know that in a sort

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:31.680
of strict constitutional sense, there is some
degree of subservience, but I'm using the

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.290
term a little bit more colloquially.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:38.950
I got emails from some people in our audience
saying, I actually respect these politicians

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.810
who are leaving because they don't want to
serve under Donald Trump.

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:44.160
That's fine.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:49.739
I'd respect them more if they stayed and actually
stood up to Trump and changed this path that

00:02:49.739 --> 00:02:53.099
the Republican Party has taken in the era
of Trumpism.

00:02:53.099 --> 00:02:57.769
Ultimately, whatever the reason is that they're
leaving, it's good in the sense that it's

00:02:57.769 --> 00:03:01.420
an opportunity for a seat to be turned democratic.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:05.760
Even if many of these are safe Republican
seats, it's still sort of early, but soon

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.440
we're going to start looking at House and
Senate 2020.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:11.349
The broad stroke is Democrats are likely to
keep the house.

00:03:11.349 --> 00:03:15.340
The question is do they extend their majority
or not?

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:19.950
Democrats are almost 100% not going to take
the Senate.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:23.050
And I know every time I mentioned this, I
get emails from people who don't seem to get

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:24.050
it.

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:28.620
It's not my opinion of the tone or direction
of the country.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.470
It's only one third of senators are up for
reelection.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:38.610
Each cycle, the seats that are up for reelection,
this cycle favor Republicans keeping control

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:39.610
of the Senate.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:43.190
I just don't see any path to Democrats taking
over the Senate in 2020.

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:47.599
The idea would be uh, try to retain the Doug
Jones seat.

00:03:47.599 --> 00:03:53.440
Don't lose more than one or two seats net
and then maybe be in a position in 2022 hopefully

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.670
with a Democratic president in the White House
at that point to take over control of the

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:58.970
Senate in the mid term.

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:00.959
That will come, uh, in three years.

00:04:00.959 --> 00:04:02.760
I guess it is at this point in time.

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:08.200
If you believe Democrats can take the Senate
in 2020 get in touch and tell me how because

00:04:08.200 --> 00:05:27.580
I simply don't see it.

